# Abstract, Chapter 1 and Chapter 2.
# Markup: <sub> <sup> <b> <i>; citations [@key, @key]; references {fig:x} {tab:x} {sec:x} {eq:x}; {n:x} gives the number only.

ABSTRACT = [
    "Mobile robots that map tunnels, mines and long corridors lose their LiDAR odometry when the scene stops constraining a direction of motion. In a corridor with smooth walls the motion along the axis is unobserved, and a camera-based estimator must carry that direction. Switching methods such as Switch-SLAM choose between LiDAR and visual odometry with one eigenvalue test on the whole registration Hessian, in which rotation (radians) and translation (metres) share one scale.",
    "This work builds a LiDAR-visual-inertial odometry front end on A-LOAM and VINS-Mono that assesses rotation and translation separately. The Schur complements of the A-LOAM Hessian give a rotation block (channel R) and a translation block (channel T), and the translation block sets a per-direction weight between the LiDAR and VINS-Mono increments. A third channel, the additional T, uses the orientation tensor of the surface normals and edge directions of the raw keyframe points to bound the translation information in each direction, and the same normals correct the heading. Versions F20 to F24 added a VINS-Mono trust check, an anchored hand-over and a guard on the initial guess of A-LOAM. The final version, F35, adopts the degeneracy status rule of Switch-SLAM, so that VINS-Mono seeds the scan-to-map step of A-LOAM only when the scan is degenerate.",
    "Accuracy is measured as the absolute trajectory error (ATE), the root-mean-square distance in metres between the estimated and true positions after the two paths are fitted to each other. Without loop closure and with frozen settings, F35 gave an ATE of 0.54 m on SubT-MRS Multi Floor and 2.10 m on Long Corridor, against 1.61 m and 2.42 m published for Switch-SLAM, and 0.29 m on a simulated farm route against 0.38 m. Its mean error was 39 to 98 % lower than that of VINS-Mono alone on all six sequences and of A-LOAM alone on the four where it was run, but on Cave02 (6.23 m) it was higher than that of an earlier version (4.4 m). With one to three live runs per sequence, only the GEODE comparison with VINS-Mono alone is significant (p = 0.008), and all sequences were used in development, so the results are not a held-out test.",
]

CH1 = [
    ('ch', 'INTRODUCTION'),
    ('h2', 'sec:background', 'Background and motivation'),
    ('p', "Autonomous mobile robots are now used to move material in factories and warehouses, to inspect tunnels, mines and pipelines, and to survey sites where it is unsafe for people to work. Unlike automated guided vehicles, which follow fixed paths, these robots plan their own routes around people and obstacles, so accurate localization is a precondition for their use [@fragapane]. Every such task requires the robot to know where it is and what its surroundings look like. Estimating both at the same time is the problem of simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) [@durrant, @cadena]."),
    ('p', "A robot with accurate SLAM can plan a path, return to a charging station and leave behind a map that people can use. A robot with poor SLAM accumulates error, which is called drift. In a long tunnel, a heading error of only 1° displaces the estimated position by about 5 m after 300 m of straight travel."),
    ('p', "SLAM systems are commonly divided into a front end and a back end [@cadena]. The front end, also called odometry, estimates the motion of the robot from one sensor measurement to the next and chains these increments into a trajectory. The back end recognises places that have been visited before (loop closure) and corrects the accumulated drift by optimising the whole trajectory. Loop closure can remove drift only after the robot has returned to a known place, so the accuracy of the front end determines the error on long paths without revisits, such as a corridor that is traversed once. This work concerns the front end, and all results in this report are obtained without loop closure."),
    ('h2', 'sec:sensors', 'Sensors for mobile robot odometry'),
    ('p', "Three sensors are common on mobile robots, and {tab:sensors} summarises their characteristics. A three-dimensional LiDAR (light detection and ranging) sensor measures the distance to the surrounding surfaces with laser pulses, so it observes the geometry of the scene directly and at metric scale. LiDAR odometry such as LOAM [@loam] and its successors [@lego, @liosam, @fastlio2] estimates the motion by registering each new scan to earlier scans or to a map, and it is accurate wherever the scene contains enough structure. A camera observes texture and appearance, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) measures angular rate and linear acceleration. Visual-inertial odometry (VIO), for example VINS-Mono [@vins], combines the two: the camera constrains the motion through tracked image features, and the IMU bridges fast motion and supplies the direction of gravity and, through the accelerometer, the metric scale."),
    ('tab', 'tab:sensors', {'widths': [1500, 2050, 2350, 2383],
                            'header': ['Sensor', 'Measures', 'Strengths', 'Typical failure conditions'],
                            'rows': [['3D LiDAR', 'Distances to surrounding surfaces (point cloud), 10 Hz in this work', 'Metric scale; independent of lighting; accurate in structured scenes', 'Corridors, tunnels and open fields, where the geometry repeats along the direction of motion'],
                                     ['Monocular camera', 'Images (appearance and texture)', 'Low cost; rich information in textured, well-lit scenes', 'Darkness, bare walls, fast rotation; no metric scale on its own'],
                                     ['IMU', 'Angular rate and linear acceleration', 'High rate; independent of the scene; direction of gravity', 'Drifts within seconds when its measurements are integrated alone']]},
     'Characteristics of the three sensors used for odometry'),
    ('p', "The sensors fail under different conditions. The LiDAR fails where the geometry repeats itself along a direction of motion, as in a corridor with smooth parallel walls or in an open field. The camera fails in darkness, in front of bare walls and during fast rotation, and the IMU alone drifts within seconds, because its measurements must be integrated twice to give position. The DARPA Subterranean Challenge showed how often the first two failures occur underground [@darpa]. Because the failure conditions of the LiDAR and the camera seldom coincide, a system that combines them can in principle remain accurate where either sensor alone would fail. How to combine them is the subject of this work."),
    ('h2', 'sec:degen', 'LiDAR degeneracy'),
    ('p', "LiDAR odometry estimates the motion between scans by registering the new scan to a map, usually by minimising point-to-plane and point-to-line distances with the Gauss-Newton or Levenberg-Marquardt method. The registration Hessian H, a 6 x 6 matrix over three rotation and three translation directions, describes how strongly the scan constrains each direction of motion. When the walls of a corridor are smooth and parallel, every plane normal is perpendicular to the corridor axis. A shift along the axis then changes none of the residuals, and H has an eigenvalue close to zero in that direction. The solver still returns an answer, but along that direction the answer is determined by noise and by the initial guess. Zhang, Kaess and Singh called this condition degeneracy and proposed to detect it from the eigenvalues of H [@zhang16]."),
    ('p', "{fig:degen} illustrates degeneracy in a corridor. The robot moves from position 1 to position 2, and the two scans are nearly identical because each contains only two flat walls. The walls fix the position of the robot across the corridor, but a scan taken further along the corridor looks the same, so the scan does not reveal how far the robot has moved."),
    ('fig', 'fig:degen', 122, 'LiDAR degeneracy in a corridor (schematic): scans taken at two positions along the corridor are nearly identical, so the motion along the corridor is not constrained'),
    ('p', "The effect is large on real data. In this work A-LOAM [@aloam], an open-source implementation of LOAM, was run on its own and gave an absolute trajectory error (ATE) of 15.8 m on the 579 m SubT-MRS Long Corridor sequence and 66 m on the 695 m Corridor02 sequence. On the Multi Floor sequence, in which a legged robot climbs stairs between floors, it gave 26.4 m. The fused estimates reported in Chapter 5 have errors of 0.5 to 2.1 m on the same three sequences."),
    ('p', "Every error in this report is expressed as the absolute trajectory error. At each instant of a run the estimator reports a position, and the dataset or the simulator provides the true position at the same instant. The ATE is the root-mean-square distance between the two, in metres, after the estimated path has been aligned with the true path as closely as possible ({sec:metrics} and {fig:ate}). For example, an ATE of 2.1 m on a path of 579 m means that the estimate typically lies about 2 m from the true position, which is 0.4 % of the distance travelled. A smaller ATE indicates a more accurate estimate."),
    ('h2', 'sec:vio', 'Visual-inertial odometry and its failure modes'),
    ('p', "A monocular VIO such as VINS-Mono [@vins] tracks image features from frame to frame and integrates the IMU measurements between frames. It does not depend on the geometry of the scene, so it continues to work in a corridor. Its scale, however, is observable only through the accelerometer, and its heading drifts slowly, because rotation about the direction of gravity cannot be observed from the IMU. It also requires texture and parallax: a pure rotation in place produces no parallax, and a dark or featureless wall produces no features. On the sequences of this work VINS-Mono alone gave mean ATEs of 1.28 m on Multi Floor, 5.81 m on Long Corridor, 4.03 m on Corridor02 and 10.24 m on Cave02, and on the GEODE stairs sequence it repeatedly lost track and reached 153 to 272 m. The two sensors are therefore complementary: the LiDAR is the better sensor where the geometry is rich, and VIO is the better sensor along a degenerate direction."),
    ('h2', 'sec:switch', 'Switching-based LiDAR-visual-inertial fusion'),
    ('p', "Switch-SLAM [@switch] runs LiDAR-inertial and visual-inertial odometry side by side and, when it detects LiDAR degeneracy, replaces the initial pose of the LiDAR odometry with the visual estimate. The detector compares the eigenvalues of the registration Hessian with a threshold derived from the semicircle law of random matrices. This design leaves room for improvement in three respects. First, the test is applied to the whole Hessian, in which rotation is measured in radians and translation in metres, so a rotation problem can be masked by the larger translation values and the reverse. Second, the decision is all or nothing: the whole pose comes from one source, although in a corridor only one translation direction is lost and the other five remain well constrained by the LiDAR. Third, the detector relies on the solver's own Hessian, and a solver that converges to a wrong minimum can still report a confident Hessian."),
    ('h2', 'sec:gap', 'Research gap'),
    ('p', "The literature reviewed in Chapter 2 offers methods that separate rotation from translation in the Hessian and methods that measure degeneracy from the scan geometry without the solver, but these methods work on the LiDAR alone. The fusion systems that add a camera, in turn, mostly switch the whole pose between sensors or use the visual estimate only as the initial guess of the LiDAR matching. No system found in the review uses rotation-translation separation together with a solver-independent geometric check to weight the LiDAR and visual-inertial motion direction by direction. Such a system would keep the LiDAR in the directions that it still constrains and hand only the degenerate direction to VIO."),
    ('h2', 'sec:problem', 'Problem statement'),
    ('p', "The problem addressed in this work is to build a LiDAR-visual-inertial odometry that (i) detects LiDAR degeneracy separately for rotation and translation by means of the Schur complements of the registration Hessian, (ii) adds a check computed from the raw scan geometry that does not depend on the solver, and (iii) uses both to weight the LiDAR and VIO motion direction by direction instead of switching between them."),
    ('h2', 'sec:objectives', 'Objectives'),
    ('p', "The objectives of the work are as follows."),
    ('li', "To separate the rotation and translation information of the A-LOAM registration Hessian with Schur complements (channels R and T) and to use the translation block to weight the LiDAR and VINS-Mono increments per direction."),
    ('li', "To add an independent channel computed from the raw keyframe geometry, the orientation tensor of surface normals and edge directions, that checks the translation information and corrects the heading (the additional T channel)."),
    ('li', "To choose the initial guess of A-LOAM's scan-to-map matching from the degeneracy status given by the rules of Switch-SLAM (version F35, the final version), protected by three safeguards: a VIO-trust check, an anchored hand-over and a guard on the mapping guess."),
    ('li', "To implement the method live in ROS on A-LOAM and VINS-Mono and to test it on the SubT-MRS Multi Floor, Long Corridor, Corridor02 and Cave02 sequences, the GEODE stairs sequence and a Gazebo farm simulation."),
    ('li', "To compare the final version with VINS-Mono alone, A-LOAM alone and the published Switch-SLAM results on Multi Floor, Long Corridor and the farm route, with frozen settings and without loop closure."),
    ('h2', 'sec:scope', 'Scope of the work'),
    ('p', "The work is limited to the odometry front end. Loop closure and map optimisation are not part of the evaluated system, and the results are compared only with published front-end results. All tests were run on a single laptop, on recorded sensor data (ROS bags) played back at real time or at a reduced rate ({sec:replay}) and on a Gazebo simulation; no physical robot was driven. The LiDAR odometry is A-LOAM without an IMU of its own, and the visual-inertial odometry is VINS-Mono with a configuration per sequence that was not tuned. The number of live runs per sequence and method is small (one to nine), so the statistical conclusions are limited, as discussed in {sec:stats}."),
    ('h2', 'sec:organisation', 'Organisation of the report'),
    ('p', "The remainder of the report is organised as follows. Chapter 2 reviews LiDAR and visual-inertial odometry, the detection of degeneracy, geometric measures from surface normals, multi-sensor fusion and the evaluation of odometry. Chapter 3 describes the method: the fusion node, the three channels and the heading correction. Chapter 4 describes the test setup, the datasets, the safeguards added after the first live tests, the final configuration and the evaluation protocol. Chapter 5 presents and discusses the results, and Chapter 6 states the conclusions and the future scope."),
]

CH2 = [
    ('ch', 'LITERATURE REVIEW'),
    ('h2', 'sec:litintro', 'Introduction'),
    ('p', "This chapter reviews the work on which the method of Chapter 3 builds. Sections {n:sec:lidarodo} and {n:sec:visodo} introduce LiDAR odometry and visual-inertial odometry. Sections {n:sec:hessian} to {n:sec:normals} review how degeneracy has been detected from the registration Hessian, how rotation and translation have been separated and how surface-normal statistics have been used as a geometry-based measure. Sections {n:sec:fusion} and {n:sec:lvifusion} review multi-sensor fusion in general and LiDAR-visual-inertial fusion in degenerate scenes in particular. Sections {n:sec:evaluation} and {n:sec:datasets} cover the evaluation of odometry and the datasets, and {sec:litsummary} summarises the research gap."),
    ('h2', 'sec:lidarodo', 'LiDAR odometry and scan registration'),
    ('p', "LiDAR odometry estimates the motion of a sensor by registering consecutive point clouds. The classical method is the iterative closest point (ICP) algorithm of Besl and McKay [@besl], which alternates between matching each point to its closest point in the reference cloud and computing the rigid transformation that minimises the distances between the matched points. Chen and Medioni [@chenmedioni] minimised the distance from each point to the tangent plane of the reference surface instead, which converges faster on surfaces with planar structure. Generalized-ICP [@segal] combined both formulations in a probabilistic framework that models the local surface around every point, and the normal distributions transform (NDT) [@biber] replaced point correspondences with a grid of local normal distributions. More recently, KISS-ICP [@kissicp] showed that a carefully designed point-to-point ICP with an adaptive correspondence threshold can match the accuracy of more complex pipelines."),
    ('p', "LOAM [@loam] made LiDAR odometry practical in real time by extracting edge and planar feature points from each scan, estimating the motion at a high rate by scan-to-scan matching and refining it at a lower rate by scan-to-map matching. A-LOAM [@aloam], which is used in this work, is a compact open-source implementation of LOAM. LeGO-LOAM [@lego] added ground segmentation for ground vehicles. LIO-SAM [@liosam] and FAST-LIO2 [@fastlio2] coupled the LiDAR tightly with an IMU, the first in a factor graph and the second in an iterated Kalman filter. All of these methods solve a nonlinear least-squares problem whose Hessian describes how well each direction of motion is constrained, and all of them degrade when the scene leaves a direction unconstrained."),
    ('h2', 'sec:visodo', 'Visual and visual-inertial odometry'),
    ('p', "Visual odometry estimates the motion of a camera from the apparent motion of image features or pixel intensities between frames [@scaramuzza11]. A single camera cannot observe metric scale, and purely visual odometry fails when the images lack texture or change too quickly. Adding an IMU addresses both problems: the accelerometer makes the scale observable, and the gyroscope bridges short periods of poor tracking. The multi-state constraint Kalman filter (MSCKF) [@mourikis] fused feature tracks and inertial measurements in an extended Kalman filter without keeping the features in the state. OKVIS [@leutenegger] and VINS-Mono [@vins] instead optimise a sliding window of keyframes, and IMU preintegration [@forster] made the inertial terms of such optimisations efficient. VINS-Mono, which is used in this work, adds a robust initialisation, relocalisation and loop closure to its sliding-window estimator, and ORB-SLAM3 [@orbslam3] provides a feature-based visual-inertial SLAM system with maps and relocalisation."),
    ('p', "Visual-inertial odometry is sensitive to the conditions of the scene and of the motion. A benchmark of monocular VIO algorithms on flying robots [@delmerico] found that accuracy, robustness and computing cost vary widely between algorithms and between sequences. Typical causes of failure are low texture, poor lighting, motion blur and pure rotation, and a monocular system that has lost its scale or its tracking must be reinitialised [@vins]. These failures occur in places different from those of the LiDAR, which is the reason for combining the two sensors."),
    ('h2', 'sec:hessian', 'Degeneracy detection from the registration Hessian'),
    ('p', "Gelfand et al. [@gelfand] analysed the stability of point-to-plane ICP through the eigenvectors of the 6 x 6 covariance of the constraints and sampled points so that weakly constrained directions are covered. Zhang, Kaess and Singh [@zhang16] defined a degeneracy factor as the smallest eigenvalue of the Hessian and projected the solution update onto the well-constrained subspace, a technique known as solution remapping. Their threshold is a constant chosen for each sensor setup. Hinduja et al. [@hinduja] carried the same idea into a factor graph by adding only the constrained directions of a registration factor, and applied it to underwater SLAM."),
    ('p', "Later work made the test less dependent on a hand-chosen threshold. Hatleskog and Alexis [@hatleskog] propagated the point noise into the eigenvalues and decided degeneracy with a probabilistic test. Nubert et al. [@nubert] learned localizability from simulated data. X-ICP [@xicp] computed the localizability of each direction from the contribution of each point and constrained the optimisation in the weak directions, and a field study [@fieldstudy] compared detection and mitigation strategies of this kind on real data from several robots."),
    ('h2', 'sec:decoupled', 'Decoupled rotation and translation degeneracy'),
    ('p', "The eigenvalues of the full Hessian mix two units. A rotation eigenvalue scales with the square of the range of the points, whereas a translation eigenvalue does not, so a threshold that suits one is wrong for the other. DCReg [@dcreg] separates the two with Schur complements: the rotation block contains the rotation information that remains after the translation has been estimated, and the translation block the reverse. On the SubT-MRS Cave02 and GEODE stairs sequences, where rotation and translation are strongly coupled, DCReg reported localization errors of a few centimetres where minimum-eigenvalue detection failed. DCReg is a registration method, however, and does not fuse a second sensor."),
    ('h2', 'sec:normals', 'Geometry-based measures from surface normals'),
    ('p', "The distribution of the surface normals in a scan describes which directions the scene can constrain, without reference to any solver. The Bingham distribution [@bingham] is the standard model for axial data such as normals, and its sufficient statistic is the second-moment orientation tensor T = (1/n) Σ n n<sup>T</sup> [@mardia]. Orientation tensors of second and fourth order are also used to describe fibre orientation in short-fibre composites [@advani]. In LiDAR odometry, LF-GICP [@lfgicp] builds a planarity-weighted normal tensor of the map and uses its smallest eigenvalue to gate a reweighting of the registration, on the argument that the Gauss-Newton Hessian can hide degeneracy. The orientation tensor as a solver-independent measure of degeneracy is therefore prior art. LF-GICP, however, uses the LiDAR alone and does not use the tensor to correct the heading."),
    ('h2', 'sec:fusion', 'Multi-sensor fusion architectures'),
    ('p', "Multi-sensor odometry systems differ in how tightly the sensors are coupled and in how the estimate is computed. In a loosely coupled system each sensor or subsystem produces its own motion estimate, and the estimates are combined afterwards. In a tightly coupled system the raw measurements of all sensors enter a single estimator. Tight coupling uses the information more completely, whereas loose coupling keeps the subsystems independent, which makes the failure of one sensor easier to detect and isolate. The estimator itself is either a filter, which keeps only the current state and its uncertainty, as in MSCKF [@mourikis] and FAST-LIO2 [@fastlio2], or a smoother, which optimises a window or the whole history of states in a factor graph [@dellaert], as in LIO-SAM [@liosam] and VINS-Mono [@vins]."),
    ('p', "In degenerate environments the choice of coupling matters. Super Odometry [@superodom] places the IMU at the centre of the estimator: the LiDAR and visual odometries supply pose constraints that bound the IMU biases, and the system was developed for the DARPA Subterranean Challenge. R3LIVE [@r3live] couples LiDAR, camera and IMU tightly through two subsystems, one LiDAR-inertial and one visual-inertial, that update a common state. The method of this work is loosely coupled at the level of motion increments: A-LOAM and VINS-Mono run as separate estimators, and the fusion node weights their increments direction by direction. This keeps the two estimators unchanged and makes the effect of each weighting rule directly visible in the logs."),
    ('h2', 'sec:lvifusion', 'LiDAR-visual-inertial fusion in degenerate scenes'),
    ('p', "Tightly coupled LiDAR-inertial odometries such as LIO-SAM [@liosam] and FAST-LIO2 [@fastlio2] use the IMU to bridge short degenerate stretches, but they do not use a camera. LVI-SAM [@lvisam] couples a visual-inertial and a LiDAR-inertial subsystem: the visual subsystem provides the initial guess for the LiDAR scan matching, and the LiDAR depth supports the visual features. RELEAD [@relead] constrains the LiDAR optimisation in directions that are not localizable and fuses it with VIO in a factor graph. ORB-SLAM3 [@orbslam3] is a feature-based visual-inertial SLAM system with maps and relocalisation, but it has no LiDAR. Switch-SLAM [@switch], described in {sec:switch}, is the system closest to this work. Its degeneracy status rule ({sec:statusguess}) is reused in the final version, and its published results without loop closure (Table II of that paper) are the targets of the comparison in Chapter 5."),
    ('h2', 'sec:evaluation', 'Evaluation of odometry accuracy'),
    ('p', "Odometry is evaluated by comparing the estimated trajectory with a ground-truth trajectory. Sturm et al. [@sturm] defined two measures that are now standard: the absolute trajectory error (ATE), which compares absolute positions after the two trajectories have been aligned, and the relative pose error, which compares the motion over fixed intervals and therefore measures drift. The KITTI benchmark [@kitti] reports relative errors as a percentage of the distance travelled. The alignment is computed with the closed-form least-squares method of Umeyama [@umeyama], with or without a scale factor, and Zhang and Scaramuzza [@zhangscara18] showed that the type of alignment must match what the estimator can observe; a system with metric sensors, for example, is aligned without scale."),
    ('p', "This work reports the ATE with a rigid alignment over the full run, together with the maximum error, as Switch-SLAM does. For comparing methods over repeated runs, Ganesan et al. [@ganesan] reported the minimum, median, maximum and mean for each environment, a success rate and one-sided hypothesis tests; the same format is used in Chapter 5."),
    ('h2', 'sec:datasets', 'Datasets for degenerate environments'),
    ('p', "Widely used odometry datasets such as KITTI [@kitti], recorded in street scenes, and EuRoC [@euroc], recorded in an industrial machine hall and a motion-capture room, were not designed around geometric degeneracy. Datasets with degenerate scenes have appeared more recently. SubT-MRS [@subtmrs] provides LiDAR, camera and IMU data from legged, wheeled and handheld platforms in tunnels, caves, corridors and stairwells, with ground-truth trajectories; its Multi Floor and Long Corridor sequences are used by Switch-SLAM. GEODE [@geode] provides data from several LiDAR types in degenerate scenes, including a stairwell with a map-based ground truth. Both are used in this work, together with a Gazebo [@gazebo] farm world; Switch-SLAM also evaluated its method on simulated Gazebo scenes, including a farm."),
    ('h2', 'sec:litsummary', 'Summary and research gap'),
    ('p', "{tab:approaches} groups the reviewed approaches by the sensors they use and states how each behaves in a degenerate scene, and {tab:lit} lists the works closest to this one."),
    ('tab', 'tab:approaches', {'widths': [1900, 2900, 3483],
                               'header': ['Approach', 'Examples', 'Behaviour in a degenerate scene'],
                               'rows': [['LiDAR odometry', 'ICP [@besl], LOAM [@loam], A-LOAM [@aloam], KISS-ICP [@kissicp]', 'Drifts or fails along the unconstrained direction'],
                                        ['Visual-inertial odometry', 'MSCKF [@mourikis], OKVIS [@leutenegger], VINS-Mono [@vins]', 'Unaffected by geometry; fails with low texture, darkness or fast rotation; heading and scale drift'],
                                        ['LiDAR-inertial odometry', 'LIO-SAM [@liosam], FAST-LIO2 [@fastlio2]', 'The IMU bridges short degenerate stretches; long stretches still drift'],
                                        ['Degeneracy-aware LiDAR registration', 'Solution remapping [@zhang16], X-ICP [@xicp], DCReg [@dcreg], LF-GICP [@lfgicp]', 'Detects weak directions and constrains or skips them; no second sensor takes over'],
                                        ['LiDAR-visual-inertial fusion', 'LVI-SAM [@lvisam], R3LIVE [@r3live], Super Odometry [@superodom], RELEAD [@relead]', 'The camera supports the LiDAR inside one estimator or as a pose constraint; detection, where present, comes from the registration'],
                                        ['Switching fusion', 'Switch-SLAM [@switch]', 'Switches the initial pose to VIO when a whole-Hessian test flags degeneracy'],
                                        ['This work', 'Channels R, T and additional T on A-LOAM and VINS-Mono', 'Weights the LiDAR and VIO per direction; the LiDAR keeps the directions it still constrains']]},
     'Odometry approaches and their behaviour in degenerate scenes'),
    ('tab', 'tab:lit', {'widths': [2000, 3300, 2983],
                        'header': ['Work', 'Contribution', 'Limitation for this problem'],
                        'rows': [['Zhang et al., 2016 [@zhang16]', 'Smallest Hessian eigenvalue as degeneracy factor; solution remapping', 'Fixed threshold; rotation and translation in one matrix'],
                                 ['Zhao et al., 2021 [@superodom]', 'IMU-centric fusion of LiDAR and visual odometry (Super Odometry)', 'No per-direction degeneracy weighting'],
                                 ['Shan et al., 2021 [@lvisam]', 'Visual-inertial guess for LiDAR matching (LVI-SAM)', 'No per-direction degeneracy weighting'],
                                 ['Tuna et al., 2024 [@xicp]', 'Per-direction localizability from point contributions (X-ICP)', 'LiDAR only; thresholds per platform'],
                                 ['Hatleskog and Alexis, 2024 [@hatleskog]', 'Noise-propagated probabilistic degeneracy test', "LiDAR only; uses the solver's Hessian"],
                                 ['Chen et al., 2024 [@relead]', 'LiDAR optimisation constrained in non-localizable directions, fused with VIO (RELEAD)', 'Detection from the registration; no solver-independent check'],
                                 ['Lee et al., 2024 [@switch]', 'Switches the LiDAR initial pose to VIO when degenerate (Switch-SLAM)', 'Whole-Hessian test; all-or-nothing switch'],
                                 ['Hu et al., 2026 [@dcreg]', 'Schur-complement separation of rotation and translation (DCReg)', 'Registration only; no camera or VIO'],
                                 ['Im, 2026 [@lfgicp]', 'Orientation tensor of map normals as a solver-independent measure', 'LiDAR only; no heading use; preprint']]},
     'Summary of the literature closest to this work'),
    ('p', "Three points emerge from the review. First, rotation and translation degeneracy have different causes and units and are better judged separately, and the Schur complement offers a principled way to do so. Second, the methods that separate them, and the methods that measure degeneracy from the scan geometry, work on the LiDAR alone. Third, the fusion systems that add a camera mostly switch the whole pose or the initial guess between sensors, and where weak directions are treated individually, as in RELEAD, the detection again comes from the registration problem itself. No system found in this review combines Schur-separated channels with an independent check from the raw geometry inside a LiDAR-visual-inertial fusion, and that combination is the subject of this work."),
]
